Consider K TCP connections, each with a different end-to-end path, but all passing through a bottleneck link with transmission rate $R$ bps. (By bottleneck link, we mean that for each connection, all the other links along the connection’s path are not congested and have abundant transmission capacity as compared with the transmission capacity of the bottleneck link.) Suppose each connection is transferring a large file and there is no UDP traffic passing through the bottleneck link. A congestion-control mechanism is said to be fair if the average transmission rate of each connection is approximately R/K;

that is, each connection gets an equal share of the link bandwidth.

Is TCP’s AIMD algorithm fair, particularly given that different TCP connections may start at different times and thus may have different window sizes at a given point in time? [Chiu 1989] provides an elegant and intuitive explanation of why TCP congestion control converges to provide an equal share of a bottleneck link’s bandwidth among competing TCP connections.

