A Bloom filter is a probabilistic data structure that can check for membership of a value in a set using very little space, but at a small risk of overestimating the set of elements that are in the set. A Bloom filter is basically a bitmap. If the set has $n$ values, the associated bitmap has a few times $n$ (typically $1 0 n$ ) bits; the Bloom filter also has associated with it several hash functions. We assume initially that there is only one hash function $h ( )$ .

The bits in the bitmap are all initially set to 0; subsequently, each value in the set is read, and the hash function $h ( 
u )$ is computed on the element $ oldsymbol { 
u }$ , with the range of the function being 1 to $1 0 n$ . The bit at position $h ( 
u )$ is then set to 1. This is repeated for every element $ oldsymbol { 
u }$ .

To check if a particular value $ oldsymbol { 
u }$ is present in the set, the hash function $h ( 
u )$ is computed. If bit $h ( 
